  <title>1.8.4: Writing Equations Given Dimensions of Shapes</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00193</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.8.4: Writing Equations Given Dimensions of Shapes</md:title>
    <md:uuid>84cc7342-b23a-4aef-b1a7-8ba8a1b72d11</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>The perimeter of a rectangle is 48 centimeters. The relationship between the length, the width, and the perimeter of the rectangle can be described with the equation: \( 2 \cdot \text{length} +2 \cdot \text{width}=48 \).</para>
<para>Find the length, in centimeters, if the width is:</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><title>Cool Down Activity</title><exercise data-content-id="7ffc205b-b5c3-4b88-9038-cd798a83705e"><problem><para>
      <para>10 centimeters
        </para>
      <para>Enter your answer here.</para>
    </para></problem><solution><para>14</para><para>
      <para>Nice try!</para>
      <para>\(2L + 2(10) = 48\)
        \(2L + 20 = 48\)
        \(2L + 20 - 20 = 48 - 20\)
        \(2L = 28\)
        \(\frac{1}{2} (2L) =\frac{1}{2} (28)\) 
        \(L = 14\)
      </para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><!--Q3-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        \( w \) centimeters
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>
      \(2L + 2(w) = 48\)
      \(2L + 2w = 48\)
      \(2L + 2w -2w= 48-2s\)
      \(2L = 48-2\)
      \(\frac{1}{2} (2L) = \frac{1}{2} (48-2s)\)
      \(L=\frac{48-2w}{2}\) or \(24-w\)
    </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
